Task 134 — post-task-118 re-measurement of the three marked-set (k/m/j/m) fingerprint
scenarios in features/kill_delete_undo.feature, per operator steering 006.

Command (per scenario, run individually and isolated, one process per run):

  docker run --rm --user "$(id -u):$(id -g)" --workdir /w \
    --mount "type=bind,src=$RALPHD_HOST_WORKSPACE,dst=/w" \
    --mount "type=volume,src=deck-go-cache,dst=/go-cache" \
    --label ralphd.run=$RALPHD_RUN_ID --label ralphd.role=sibling \
    deck-ci:local sh -c 'DECK_GODOG_TAGS="@requirement-29-<tag>" go test -count=1 -run TestFeatures ./features/'

Host state during measurement: `uptime` load average 6.18 -> 7.28 (5-10 min window),
28 cores, 3 other docker containers running alongside the sibling under test. Task 118
(commit 465a7d9, the coalesced multi-rune KeyMsg dispatch fix) is completed and present
in the tree at HEAD (465a7d9) for every run below.

@requirement-29-bulk-kill  — 10 consecutive isolated runs: OK FAIL OK FAIL FAIL FAIL FAIL FAIL OK FAIL
  => 3/10 passed, 7/10 failed.
  Task 113's own validation and its follow-up commit (0cc8c4a) claimed 10/10 consecutive
  isolated runs for this exact scenario at the time it was measured. That claim does NOT
  reproduce now: this scenario is no more reliable than the other two under today's host
  conditions, contradicting the "hardening applied identically... bulk-kill's own 10/10"
  framing in task 113's notes and in docs/reports/phase3.md's requirement-29 row.

@requirement-29-bulk-delete — 10 consecutive isolated runs: OK FAIL FAIL OK OK FAIL FAIL FAIL OK OK
  => 5/10 passed, 5/10 failed.

@requirement-29-batch-undo — 10 consecutive isolated runs: OK OK FAIL OK OK FAIL OK OK OK OK
  => 8/10 passed, 2/10 failed.

Failure signature is IDENTICAL across all three scenarios (captured verbatim from one
bulk-kill failure and one bulk-delete failure; batch-undo's failures show the same shape):

  after scenario hook failed: client "A" did not show "> bk-two running" within 5s:
  timed out waiting for frame "> bk-two running": context deadline exceeded

The captured raw frame at the moment of the timeout shows the cursor marker never moved
off the first marked row (e.g. "> bk-one running [marked]" is still current) — i.e. the
single "j" navigation keystroke sent between the two "m" mark keystrokes was never
registered by the program. This is a single-rune keystroke drop, NOT a multi-rune
KeyMsg-coalescing event (task 118's fix only ever had multi-rune KeyRunes messages to
split; a lone "j" arriving in its own PTY read is exactly one rune and was never at risk
of being silently dropped by the pre-118 switch, since msg.String() would have been "j"
and matched the existing case cleanly). Task 118 did not touch this failure mode and was
never expected to, per steering 005's own scoping of the coalescing defect to multi-rune
KeyMsg values.

Conclusion: this is the residual, environmental PTY-keystroke-delivery-loss gap that
steering 006 asked to be measured and reported "separately" from the coalescing defect —
and it is NOT confined to bulk-delete/batch-undo as task 113's validation observed at the
time. Under today's host conditions it reproduces just as severely on bulk-kill, the one
scenario previously evidenced at 10/10. The gap is shared equally across all three
k/m/j/m marked-set scenarios, is host-load-correlated (same class as the pre-existing
create_cwd_ghost.feature tilde-expansion flake and the task-114-fixed harness.feature
falls-silent flake — a family of PTY-timing races this environment already exhibits, not
unique to this task's scenarios), and is not something a poll-on-render checkpoint can
paper over: the checkpoint (`Then ... screen contains "> bk-two running"`) IS the
poll-on-render checkpoint already in place; it fails loudly and correctly exactly as
intended when the keystroke is genuinely lost upstream of the assertion. There is no
further test-side hardening available that would not violate steering 006 point 4
(no sleep-lengthening, timeout-widening, retries, tagging, checkpoint removal, marked-set
shrinking, non-keystroke marking, or scenario deletion).

Decision: option (b) per steering 006 — the residual gap is judged not worth closing in
this task (it is a harness/PTY-delivery characteristic of this host, not a product defect
in internal/tui's dispatch code, which task 118 already fixed for the class of bug it
covers). Task 113 relabelled `skipped`; docs/reports/phase3.md's requirement-29 row
downgraded off DONE to PARTIAL in the same commit, with the "every path has one [reliable]
assertion" overclaim removed.
